{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790515376,"nonce":"/BgUR36xAAkdKhBQOU/6IcRLyy4=","message":"Plan, 2026-09-27 (user: the WebIDL is codegen'd to JS for the browser host, lighter than shipping wgpu as wasm). In the browser the plugin has no wgpu. A web backend encodes each WebGPU call into a command ring in the program's shared memory, and a GPU agent (a Worker holding the GPUDevice and an OffscreenCanvas) decodes and runs it. Both halves are generated by caribou-bindgen from spec/webgpu.idl.\n\nWire format:\n- Integers and floats at their IDL widths, little-endian.\n- boolean as u8.\n- DOMString/USVString as u32 length plus UTF-8.\n- enum as a u32 index into tables generated on both sides.\n- An interface reference as its u32 handle. The plugin's own handles.rs ids are used, assigned by the plugin, so creating a resource needs no round trip: the command carries the id its result is stored under.\n- A dictionary as a presence mask plus the members present, so absent members stay absent and WebGPU applies its defaults.\n- sequence as a count plus items; record as a count plus pairs; nullable as a flag; union as a u8 alternative plus the value.\n- BufferSource/AllowSharedBufferSource as (address, length) in shared memory, read by the agent as a view without copying.\n- Promise results and attribute reads come back through a reply slot the caller waits on atomically.\n\nOrder, as issues:\n(1) bindgen: parse the full IDL and generate the wire, a Rust encoder plus a JS decoder, tested in Node against a recording mock;\n(2) cb_gpu web backend: the backend's functions over the encoder;\n(3) the GPU agent and its hook in Ash's browser host;\nplus the prerequisites: b79ee6a (threaded runtime) and 38921da (futures in compiled programs).","files":null}]}